Z||000006||Monkeyman||05-17-2000||01:23 PM||||Mainly because it's $160.  I don't have much cash right now.  I'm still trying to figure out how I'm gonna buy $150 worth of stereo speakers, etc.  (Not that that's the #1 priority, but it was when I thought I could do the headliner myself.)  I just spent almost $500 on my motor and a little over a grand getting it installed.  I'd love to be able to afford the headliner, but I'm not sure I can justify it now.  Know what I mean, Verne?  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Carrolles||05-17-2000||01:55 PM||||I have never sealed a headliner prior to recovering and have never had a problem.  New fabric not sticking is usually due either to not having removed the old foam from the fiber board or to using the wrong adhesive.  I use a broom to sweep off the old foam.  It usually just balls up and sweeps away.<P>Generic spray adhesives don't work very well.  They let go in a day or so.  If you use a spray adhesive you need the kind made for foam backed fabrics such as 3M Foamfast 74.  It sprays on like silly string and will look like a spider web when applied.  Apply to the headliner board and to the back of the fabric.  You'll have about ten minutes to position the fabric and work out wrinkles before the glue sets up.  I got mine at Hancock Fabrics where I got the headliner fabric.  The can cost around $10.00 and was enough to do the headliner in my Fiero and in my 89 Bonneville.  I also recovered the sunvisors using the spray and sewing up the edges.<P>I'd guess that you can salvage your fabric and simply re-glue it with the proper adhesive.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||05-17-2000||02:27 PM||||Thanx.  That's the way I'm gonna have to go, at least for now.  I'd like to get some speakers for the car soon.  I have a (very) long list of to-dos but most of them will have to wait for a while.  I'm trying to do as much as I can myself so I can get more done with what little money I have.  It's frustrating, but I know it will pay off.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Phil||05-17-2000||04:39 PM||||Read in one of the posts that a coat of fiberglass resin works well to seal and stiffen the board||||reg||1
Z||000010||Monkeyman||05-17-2000||08:46 PM||||Thanx, Phil.  I'll have to do a search for the thread tomorrow.  I've never worked with fiberglass before, but I guess now is as good a time as any to learn.||||reg||1
Z||000011||fierodave||05-18-2000||05:49 AM||||Just take off theold and toss it noe  for the $7.50 a yard for new  why bother with the old  yes you can brush off the old glue and crap  and the head linner may stay up for 6 months!  been there done it<BR>before you put the new one on  take a quart of the fast drying urathane wood floor sealer like they use on nice oak floors, and pout it all over it and brush it in!  do it to both sides  use the whole quart!<BR>Let it dry over night  them install like normal,  the coating will stiffen up the board to like rock hard, plus you rum less risk of breaking it putting it in!<BR>you could fiber Glass it,  but why deal with the mess ?  I have done over 20 (five in the last week for fiero's) of them in GM cars in the last 12 months <BR>It seems to be the best way I have found!<BR>PLUS  if your roof does leak a bit or you leave it open and it rains in the car and gets the headliner wet, <BR>with out sealing it!  that yellow material from the board WILL stain the new headliner!  With MY method if it gets wet! so what!  <BR>It won't stain! <BR>plus you can clean off finger marks from the kids french fry fights<BR>or you drunk uncles olympia beer can mishaps<BR>just my 2 cents<BR>FD||||reg||1
Z||000012||Monkeyman||05-18-2000||09:58 AM||||FD--If you've done a ton of them using the urethane wood floor sealer, I guess that's the way to go.  I'll head to Walmart either this afternoon or 1st thing in the am.  Thanx.||||reg||1
Z||000013||pollock||05-18-2000||11:09 AM||||Jeez, guys.......<P>Junkyard Fiero headliner board $5<BR>Taking your board to have a shop cover it $35<BR>Taking your Fiero to a shop to have them do the whole job, with a warranty, $89.<P>DIY screwups, money can't buy......<BR>For everything else, there's mastercard<P>I used to work at a headliner shop.  We never coated the boards with anything.  Good idea though, if the board is weak.  But the 3M pro adhesive we used stuck just fine with no coating.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by pollock (edited 05-18-2000).]||||reg||3
Z||000014||Monkeyman||05-18-2000||04:33 PM||||I agree that it would be much much simpler to either buy a headliner already made (Mr. Mikes or the Fiero Store) or have one done professionally, but the whole problem  here is cash.  I just don't have any right now.  Maybe in a couple of months I could afford that, but I really want to put my interior back together.  Thanx for the suggestion, though.||||reg||1
Z||000015||BOILERMAKER||05-18-2000||10:41 PM||||Monkey, On the back half of the sunroof opening on the headliner make sure you leave up to 2 inches of overlap (you can always trim off the extra).  I just got done doing mine and didn't leave enough.  The rest of the sunroof opening looks good except for the rear.  <P>I guess I will have to do it over again, but there again this one is my beater, so I may wait a little longer.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Monkeyman||05-19-2000||10:14 AM||||Unfortunately, it doesn't matter anymore.  I went to Wally World after work this am and got the urethane just like FD said, came home and (expletive deleted) I found that my headliner had fallen during the night and is now in 3 pieces.  I guess I'll put the rest of the interior back together until I can come up with $160 for one of those fancy fiberglass ones.  This will <B>not</B> be a top priority, but maybe in the spring??||||reg||1
